  "dom_id": "step-q136342",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The velocity of a particle is $(5i+j)\\,\\text{km/h}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{h}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $3j\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{h}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=5i+j,\\,a=3j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=4\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(5i+j)+(3j)(4) \\\\  & =(5i+j)+(12j) \\\\  & =(5i+13j)\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
